uction to apply even pressure to paper. And he created an oil-based ink that adhered properly to type and paper. This combination allowed for the efficient, repeatable reproduction of text with a consistency that hand-copying could never achieve.\n\nThe most famous product of Gutenberg's press was the Gutenberg Bible, produced around 1455. This four-volume work demonstrated the press's capacity for producing beautiful, uniform texts. Yet the Bible was only the beginning. The press quickly turned to cheaper, more practical works, and by the end of the"}